Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 03/16/2023]
1.	Direct capture estimate: weighted average of C2S values from Polsky et al. 1969, Landre et al. 1989, Kontos et al. 2012. DC potential model 
    reproduces the DC S factor from fit on low-energy data in Buckner et al. 2015 within 20%. Assume uncertainty of 20% for total DC S factor.
2.  Ercm=183 keV: wg from Buckner et al. 2014, Di Leva et al. 2014, Chafa et al. 2005 [see Tab. II of Buckner et al. 2015].
3.  Ercm=490 keV: see Tab. II of Buckner et al. 2015.
4.  Ercm>501 keV: wg values are a weighted average of Rolfs et al. 1973 [their renormalized values, see Fox et al. 2005] and Kontos et al. 2012.
5.  Ercm=556 keV and 677 keV: partial widths from R-matrix analysis of Kontos et al. 2012, but their tiny uncertainties on Gg [about 1%] have 
    been inflated to match uncertainties of their measured resonance strengths; their partial widths agree with their directly measured (p,g) 
    resonance strengths.
6.  Final state energy [after primary gamma-ray transition] is determined by strongest primary branching [Tilley et al. 1995; Kontos et al. 2012].
7.  Ercm= -3.1 keV: Gg and Ga from Mak et al. 1980; C2S from Landre et al. 1989.
8.  Ercm= -1.6 keV: Gg and Ga from Mak et al. 1980; C2S upper limit from transfer work of Landre et al. 1989; the much smaller value from the DC 
    work of Rolfs & Rodney 1975 (C2S<0.0003) is not trustworthy considering that for the Ercm= -3.1 keV level the transfer work yields C2S=0.12+-0.04, 
    while the DC work gives C2S<0.002. Value of the mean reduced width, needed for the Porter-Thomas distribution (0.001, with factor 5 uncertainty) 
    is estimated from Pogrebnyak et al. 2013. For more information, see discussion in Buckner et al. 2015.
9.  Ercm=65 keV: partial widths from measured (p,a) [Bayesian weighted average from Blackmon et al. 1995; Hannam and Thompson 1999; Bruno et al. 2016] 
    and (a,g) resonance strengths [Parker 1968; Rolfs et al. 1973; Berka et al. 1977; Becker et al. 1982], and from Ga [14N(a,a) R-matrix analysis of 
    Mak et al. 1980].
10. Strengths of resonances with energies in excess of those listed here are reported in ENSDF 1994, but their absolute magnitudes are questionable.
11. Tests [using "+" interference; see Fig. 9 in Kontos et al 2012] showed that interference between 2+ resonances at 676 keV and 778 keV is negligible 
    for total reaction rates; thus only their incoherent sum is considered here.




